# Odoo 19 Development Rules

This document defines development rules specific to **Odoo 19**.

It complements the general rules defined in:

ai-rules/odoo/odoo_common.md

Developers and AI agents must ensure that generated code is fully compatible with Odoo 19.


--------------------------------------------------
View Architecture
--------------------------------------------------

Odoo 19 replaces the `<tree>` view tag with `<list>`.

Incorrect (deprecated):

<tree>
    <field name="name"/>
</tree>

Correct:

<list>
    <field name="name"/>
</list>


--------------------------------------------------
Deprecated Attributes
--------------------------------------------------

The following attributes should **not be used in Odoo 19**.


attrs

The `attrs` attribute used for dynamic field behavior is deprecated.

Incorrect:

<field name="date_done" attrs="{'invisible': [('state','=','draft')]}"/>

Instead, use explicit attributes with expressions.


states

The `states` attribute on fields is deprecated.

Incorrect:

states={'done': [('readonly', True)]}


statusbar_colors

The attribute `statusbar_colors` is deprecated and must not be used.


--------------------------------------------------
Field Visibility
--------------------------------------------------

Use explicit attributes instead of deprecated dynamic attributes.

Allowed attributes:

invisible  
readonly  
required  
widget  
options


Example:

<field name="date_done" invisible="state != 'done'"/>


--------------------------------------------------
Widgets
--------------------------------------------------

Only use widgets compatible with Odoo 19.

Common widgets include:

many2one  
many2many_tags  
boolean_toggle  
badge  
statusbar  
html  
url  
daterange


Before introducing a custom widget, verify it exists in Odoo 19.


--------------------------------------------------
OWL Framework
--------------------------------------------------

Odoo 19 uses OWL (Odoo Web Library) for frontend components.

Guidelines:

- prefer existing widgets when possible
- avoid unnecessary custom components
- keep frontend logic minimal


--------------------------------------------------
Server Actions
--------------------------------------------------

Server actions should use the standard Odoo structure.

Example:

<record id="action_server_my_action" model="ir.actions.server">
    <field name="name">My Action</field>
    <field name="model_id" ref="model_sigop_work_order"/>
    <field name="state">code</field>
    <field name="code">
action = records.action_my_method()
    </field>
</record>


--------------------------------------------------
Scheduled Actions (Cron)
--------------------------------------------------

Scheduled tasks must use ir.cron records.

Example:

<record id="cron_check_extensions" model="ir.cron">
    <field name="name">Check Expired Extensions</field>
    <field name="model_id" ref="model_sigop_work_order_extension"/>
    <field name="state">code</field>
    <field name="code">model._cron_check_extensions()</field>
    <field name="interval_number">1</field>
    <field name="interval_type">days</field>
</record>


--------------------------------------------------
ORM API Compatibility
--------------------------------------------------

Always verify ORM usage against the official documentation:

https://www.odoo.com/documentation/19.0/developer/reference/backend/orm.html

Avoid patterns from older versions unless verified.


--------------------------------------------------
Security Rules
--------------------------------------------------

All models must define access rules in:

security/ir.model.access.csv

Without these rules, users will receive access errors.


--------------------------------------------------
Module Structure
--------------------------------------------------

Follow the standard Odoo module layout.

Example:

models/
views/
security/
data/
static/


Each model should typically have:

- a Python model file
- a corresponding view file


--------------------------------------------------
Upgrade Awareness
--------------------------------------------------

When writing code, avoid patterns that could break in future versions.

Prefer:

- simple views
- explicit logic
- minimal frontend customization


--------------------------------------------------
Reference Documentation
--------------------------------------------------

Official documentation:

https://www.odoo.com/documentation/19.0/

Developer documentation:

